    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d172-a1">

                <pb facs="0211" n="161" sameAs="frus:frus1894/pg161"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Gresham to Mr.
                            Carlisle.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-03-12"><hi rendition="#italic">March 12,
                        1894</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to send you for your
                    information copy of a note just received from the Chinese minister at this
                    capital relative to difficulties encountered by his countrymen in registering
                    under the Geary law. If the statements in this note are correct, and I have no
                    knowledge tending to show that they are not, would it not be well to increase
                    the facilities for registering? I venture to suggest further the propriety of
                    sending officers to the Chinese camps in the mountains that they need not be
                    required to incur the heavy expense of traveling to some distant point and
                    remaining there indefinitely for an opportunity to register.</p>
                <p>You will oblige me by replying to this letter at your early convenience, in order
                    that I may make proper acknowledgment of the communication just received.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. Q. Gresham.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
